## Technical FAQs

**Q: Why an IC50 threshold of 1000 nM to define "active"?**

A: IC50 < 1000 nM (pIC50 > 6) is a widely used cutoff in kinase and general QSAR benchmarks. It is a convention, not a biological boundary: molecules near 1000 nM are close to a coin flip in label terms, and 1,046 structures have records on both sides of the threshold. In this dataset 66.7% of molecules are active.

**Q: What does "inactive" mean here?**

A: An IC50 of 1000 nM or higher, either measured exactly or established by a bound such as "IC50 > 10,000 nM". These are molecules that were synthesized and tested, often analogs from optimization series, not "does not bind". A real screening library contains mostly untested molecules that look different, so this dataset is not representative of a typical screening library.

**Q: How did you handle censored measurements such as "IC50 > 1000 nM"?**

A: They are used only when the bound already settles the label ("IC50 > x" with x >= 1000 nM is inactive, "IC50 < x" with x <= 1000 nM is active). Other censored records are dropped. Treating them as exact values would corrupt the labels, and dropping all of them would remove many clearly inactive compounds (an exact-only version of the dataset was 73.2% active, against 66.7% in the final version).

**Q: How did you handle multiple measurements and duplicate structures?**

A: Structures were standardized and merged by the connectivity block of the InChIKey, so salts, charge states and stereoisomers collapse into one row (13,302 ChEMBL IDs became 12,888 structures). Each structure is labelled by the majority of its records, and 463 exact ties were dropped. Values for the same compound can differ by orders of magnitude across assays (for example 41, 300 and 7,820 nM). Assay type and EGFR mutant status are not modelled, and the target ID CHEMBL203 can include measurements on mutant forms. This is a known limitation.
**Q: How did you handle multiple measurements for the same molecule?**

A: IC50 values from different assays can differ by orders of magnitude
(for example 41, 300 and 7,820 nM for the same compound). I used the
median per molecule, which is robust to single outlier assays, and
removed values below 0.01 nM or above 1,000,000 nM as implausible.
Assay-level differences (cell-based vs enzymatic, wild-type vs mutant
EGFR) were not modelled, and the target ID CHEMBL203 can include
measurements on mutant forms. This is a known limitation.

**Q: Why Morgan fingerprints with radius 2 and 2048 bits?**

A: Morgan fingerprints (ECFP-like) are a standard, strong baseline for
activity prediction. Radius 2 captures substructures up to 2 bonds
around each atom, and 2048 bits is a common size that balances
information and dimensionality. I did not tune these values, so other
settings (for example radius 3, counts instead of bits) could perform
differently.

**Q: Are there any information losses in a 2048-bit fingerprint?**

A: Yes. Different substructures can hash to the same bit (collisions),
and the fingerprint ignores 3D shape and stereochemistry details. This
is acceptable for a baseline, and it is one reason a final docking step
is planned: it adds structural information the fingerprint lacks.

**Q: Did you standardize the molecules before computing features?**

A: SMILES were used as provided by ChEMBL (all 13,518 parsed without
errors in RDKit). I did not apply extra standardization such as salt
stripping, neutralization or tautomer canonicalization, so a small
fraction of molecules may be represented in non-parent forms. Adding a
standardization step is listed as an improvement.

**Q: Why these six descriptors?**

A: They are the Lipinski-related properties (molecular weight, LogP,
H-bond donors and acceptors, TPSA, rotatable bonds), chosen because
they are interpretable and relevant to drug-likeness. Active compounds
showed modestly higher molecular weight, LogP, acceptor count and
rotatable bonds, but no single descriptor separates the classes.


**Q: Why Random Forest and XGBoost?**

A: Both are strong, well-established baselines on fingerprint data and
require little tuning. They also allow a clear comparison between
bagging and boosting. Graph neural networks are a reasonable next step
at this dataset size (about 13.5k molecules), and were left as future
work to keep the scope manageable. Fingerprint plus tree-model baselines
are often hard to beat on datasets of this size.

**Q: Did you tune hyperparameters?**

A: No. Both models use reasonable fixed settings (500 trees, XGBoost
max_depth 6 and learning rate 0.1) and class weighting. Tuning with
cross-validation inside the training set would likely give small gains.
The test set was used only for final evaluation.

**Q: Is Random Forest really better than XGBoost?**

A: Random Forest scored higher on both splits (0.945 vs 0.938 random,
0.890 vs 0.868 scaffold), but this comes from a single split and a
single random seed, without confidence intervals. The result is
suggestive, not conclusive. Repeating with several seeds and scaffold
folds would be needed to claim a real difference.

**Q: Why ROC-AUC, and does 0.89 mean the model would find new EGFR inhibitors?**

A: ROC-AUC is threshold-independent and handles the moderate class
imbalance. It measures how well the model separates measured actives
from measured inactives within this dataset. It does not directly
measure screening performance on a library of untested molecules, which
is why the next notebook reports enrichment on a decoy library instead.

**Q: Why did you drop the descriptors if they slightly improved AUC?**

A: Adding descriptors to fingerprints changed scaffold-split AUC from
0.890 to 0.892, a difference within expected variation. Descriptors
alone reached 0.744. I kept fingerprints only for simplicity. This
choice was made after looking at test-set results, but with a 0.002
difference the risk of selection bias is negligible.

**Q: Why did you evaluate on a scaffold split in addition to a random split, and why are the AUCs different?**

A: In a random split, structurally similar molecules from the same
chemical series end up in both the training and test sets, so the model
is partly rewarded for recognizing near-duplicates. A scaffold split
places entire chemical families in one set only, which mimics the real
use case of screening new compounds. The AUC drop from 0.945 to 0.890
(Random Forest) quantifies this optimism. Both results are reported,
and the scaffold split is treated as the primary estimate.
